+<p>Fair claiming does not preempt a long handler or guarantee a fixed wall-clock response time. The single local worker still requires short/cooperative preparation or a separate qualified compute adapter for heavy work.</p>
+<h2>11. Disablement, migration and upgrades</h2>
+<p><code>enabled</code> permits authorized reads and writes. <code>read-only</code> retains permitted history but denies new commands and pauses new job claims. <code>off</code> denies the module's operations and removes its workspace. The code does not confuse those modes with package installation or data purge.</p>
+<p>Migration history is hashed and ordered. Editing an applied migration or opening a newer unsupported generation is rejected. Reader/writer changes require owner-specific evolution. Removal assessment reports dependents, retained records and pending jobs; destructive uninstall, data purge, global rollback and historical-reader migration are <strong>not implemented</strong>.</p>
+<p>Handoff migration 1 remains byte-identical; migration 2 preserves old protocol rows and adds delivery facts. Existing pending v1 commands are not automatically retried through v2, whose receipt namespace is different. They require original-protocol reconciliation. New permissions and bindings are explicit; existing seeded principals are not silently regranted.</p>
+<p>Package versions and per-product build records support explicit adoption. Two actual 0.1/0.2 local neutral runtimes were exercised independently, with the older executable and reported version unchanged. This is narrower than published package adoption or shared-data rolling migration. This release does not claim a tested rolling upgrade between two independently published framework versions. Exported products contain fixed package versions; they are artifacts of the maintained source, not a recommendation to edit forked shell copies.</p>
+<h2>12. Verification and admission to broader use</h2>
+<p>Actual checks cover exact composition and provider ordering, owner transactions, migrations, role/tenant negatives, job restart and fencing, queue fairness, an independent worker process, local and HTTP cross-product commands, typed receipts and failure-state preservation, receipt-only reconciliation, multi-file asset closure, neutral export, a generated module without host edits, and a worker-store replacement with SQLite absent. Shell transitions run against actual compiled code using controlled document/event/request ports, not a real browser.</p>
+<p>The next engineering work is to complete real browser/React acceptance, production identity/storage/runtime adapters, package publication/version adoption, resource limits for real workloads, operational lifecycle and a selected consumer adapter for existing Veyviz. Do not replace Veyviz's owners, grant model access, or declare production readiness from this developer-profile proof.</p>
+<h2>13. Remediation records</h2>
+<p>The R01–R10 implementation map, regression identities and limits are in <code>docs/REMEDIATION.md</code>. Source compatibility, legacy pending commands and new explicit permissions are described in <code>docs/UPGRADE_FROM_0_1.md</code>. These updates refine the same framework rather than introduce new business owners.</p>
