+<h2>Cross-product state is three-dimensional</h2>
+<ol>
+<li><strong>Delivery fact:</strong> original destination/command, dispatch uncertainty, validated receipt.</li>
+<li><strong>Technical job:</strong> queued/running/failed/cancelled/expired/succeeded with attempts and stop reason.</li>
+<li><strong>Source workflow:</strong> the Handoff owner's adopted business state.</li>
+</ol>
+<p>A destination receipt can be retained after local write authority or a worker lease is lost. Its fact-only recorder can update only that existing delivery's receipt; it cannot dispatch another request or mark business completion. The normal worker or explicit <code>handoff.reconcile</code> operation must still satisfy current owner authority before adopting the source outcome.</p>
+<p>An UNKNOWN record remains UNKNOWN when retries expire. A read-only destination lookup uses the original actor/tenant/command key and requires <code>desk.receipts.read</code>. Lookup absence/error does not prove no prior commit. Receipt retrieval grants no ordinary destination record-list or document access.</p>
+<p>Connection identity includes implementation/binding revision, actual product/instance, service actor, contract, tenant mapping and destination path/origin. Credential bytes are excluded from the binding identity: rotating a token for the same principal need not retarget an operation. Changing the principal or destination cannot silently replay an unresolved operation under a different receipt namespace.</p>
+<h2>Scheduling bounds and their limits</h2>
+<p>The host rotates among ready module stores. The SQLite adapter rotates tenant partitions and scans at most 16 due tenant partitions and 100 candidate jobs per selected partition per claim. Cursor progress is persisted. These are local implementation defaults, not throughput/SLA guarantees.</p>
+<p>Claim fairness does not preempt a long handler. A synchronous blocking handler can still delay a single-process worker; native/CPU-heavy work requires the separate execution profile already described in the architecture. Lease fencing is not proof of external-action reversal or physical compute termination.</p>
+<h2>Frontend profile and replacement proof</h2>
+<p>The supported closure includes static imports/exports, literal <code>import()</code>, literal <code>new URL(..., import.meta.url)</code>, and <code>browserAssets</code> copied from the module's source tree. CSS dependencies are included. Absolute/unselected/server-only imports and nonliteral dynamic imports fail build. This is a bounded native-ESM profile, not a general-purpose replacement for Vite/Webpack or a complete React build adapter.</p>
+<p>The second worker-store implementation is <strong>ephemeral test/conformance code</strong>. It proves the host's dependency boundary, rollback behavior and async compatibility without SQLite. It does not establish production persistence, distributed queue semantics or durability after process loss.</p>
+<h2>Compatibility</h2>
+<p>Framework packages and product definitions select 0.2.0. Module ABI naming remains v1, but 0.1 framework package mixing is not promised. Public Service Desk v1 stays available; v2 is explicit for new integrations. Handoff schema migration 1 is unchanged; migration 2 preserves old data and blocks legacy v1 automatic resubmission. Read <a href="docs/UPGRADE_FROM_0_1.md">UPGRADE_FROM_0_1.md</a>.</p>
