+<p>All ten findings have implemented corrections and local regressions. Real browser/React acceptance, published-package adoption, production identity/storage/recovery, native workloads and Veyviz integration remain separate.</p>
+</section><section id="upgrade"><div class="eyebrow">FOUNDATION 0.2 · Upgrade safely</div><h1>Adopting Foundation 0.2 from 0.1</h1>
+<h2>Recommended path</h2>
+<p>Extract the complete 0.2 archive into a <strong>new directory</strong>. Run <code>npm run setup</code>, then <code>npm run demo</code> with new local data. Keep the original source and data intact. This is a local framework preview, not an automatically managed production upgrade.</p>
+<p>The accompanying source patch is for the exact original 0.1 archive, not an arbitrary checkout. Use <code>git apply --check</code> first and review changes. The patch does not contain refreshed compiled output: run the build using the pinned development tools before starting patched source. The complete archive already contains the updated compiled products.</p>
+<h2>Package and browser compatibility</h2>
+<p>All maintained packages, reference modules and selected product definitions are 0.2.0. Rebuild an exact composition; do not mix 0.1 clients with 0.2 framework packages merely because the module ABI retains its v1 name. There is no independent published-package rolling-upgrade guarantee.</p>
+<p>The browser loader now honors the exact <code>export</code> name. Modules can use selected multi-file native ESM and declared assets. Unsupported bare browser imports fail build rather than being exposed through the server. Existing single-workspace examples remain valid.</p>
+<h2>Explicit destination contract</h2>
+<p>Existing human <code>GET/POST /api/requests</code> and <code>desk.requests.create.v1</code> remain. New cross-product wiring selects:</p>
+<ul>
+<li><code>desk.requests.create.v2</code> and <code>POST /api/requests/commit</code> for an explicit COMMITTED receipt;</li>
+<li><code>GET /api/requests/receipt?commandId=...</code> for <strong>lookup only</strong>;</li>
+<li>exact destination product, instance, tenant mapping and destination service actor;</li>
+<li>original command identity preserved across retries and receipt reads.</li>
+</ul>
+<p><code>desk.receipts.read</code> permits only the authenticated actor's original v2 receipts in its permitted tenant. It does not grant normal <code>desk.read</code>. <code>handoff.reconcile</code> permits the source's explicit reconciliation operation. The fresh local example seeds these grants; <strong>existing seeded principals are not silently changed</strong>. An operator must review and deliberately apply any local grant/configuration migration, or use a separate clean preview data directory. Do not hand-copy secrets into source.</p>
+<h2>Existing local data</h2>
+<p>Service Desk's existing migration is unchanged. Handoff migration 1 is unchanged. Handoff migration 2 adds a protocol marker and an owner-local delivery-fact table. Queue metadata adds a separate stop reason and scheduling cursors. Tests exercise old schema preservation and refusal of edited migration history.</p>
+<p>These migrations do not supply a backup/restore system. Preserve a consistent stopped local data copy before experimenting with existing data. Opening migrated Handoff data with old 0.1 code is not supported and must not be called rollback.</p>
+<h3>Legacy pending cross-product operations</h3>
+<p>A v1 destination command may already have committed even when the old source records no receipt. <strong>Do not send that old operation through v2:</strong> the receiver's operation/receipt namespace changed, so it could create a second resource.</p>
+<p>The 0.2 source retains those v1 rows and reports <code>RECONCILIATION_REQUIRED</code>; already accepted v1 rows retain their target references and are labelled legacy. It does not fabricate a v2 receipt, silently rewrite the old command identity or automatically resend.</p>
+<p>Resolve a legacy pending operation through its original receiver/actor/tenant/command protocol and an explicit reviewed migration. An automated legacy reconciliation tool is not included. This deliberate hold is safer than duplicating a cross-product business action.</p>
+<h3>New v2 operations</h3>
+<p>A recorded COMMITTED receipt can coexist with a failed/cancelled local job. UNKNOWN remains after timeout, cancellation or exhaustion. The authorized <strong>Check original receipt</strong> action checks the original command without resending. Source business completion still passes its owner command and current authority. Technical attempt history is not rewritten to look successful.</p>
+<p>Changing a destination binding or service principal for a pending operation requires reconciliation rather than retargeting it. Same-principal credential rotation is distinct from choosing another identity.</p>
+<h2>What the version exercise proves</h2>
+<p><code>tools/version-isolation.mjs --older-root &lt;original-0.1-directory&gt;</code> runs actual old/new neutral snapshots in separate temporary local instances and verifies the older runtime stays on 0.1 with unchanged executable bytes when 0.2 starts.</p>
+<p>It does <strong>not</strong> prove published package distribution, shared-data migration rollback, browser compatibility, production rolling upgrades or fleet operation. See <code>evidence/version_isolation.json</code>.</p>
