+<p>This is a consumer integration example. Install/build the consumer's real React packages using its toolchain. The neutral foundation does not bundle a React version. The provided port-lifecycle checks are not React runtime proof.</p>
+<h2>Worker handler</h2>
+<p>Worker registrations use <code>JobExecutionStore</code> from the neutral contracts package. The host does not import SQLite. Implement <code>claim</code>, <code>renew</code>, <code>assertCurrent</code>, <code>finish</code> and <code>fail</code>, retaining the adapter-owned atomic completion relationship. These calls may be async; the local reference adapter stays synchronous. The independent memory adapter under <code>tests/fixtures</code> is ephemeral conformance code, not a replacement production database.</p>
+<p>Prepare expensive work outside the transaction. Return a short <code>commit()</code> callback that applies its owner-local result. The worker verifies its current lease and authority before that callback. Use stable identities for external effects that may occur before commit; never claim a database transaction can undo a remote operation. The handler receives a current job/attempt check; invoke it immediately before an external dispatch and durably mark uncertainty. Record received external facts separately before a source business completion that may be refused. Read-only reconciliation must not submit another effect.</p>
+<p>The reference scheduler rotates modules and tenant partitions with bounded advancing eligibility scans. This prevents the reviewed starvation but does not preempt a long-running handler or guarantee latency under arbitrary load.</p>
+<p>The reference index is bounded text processing. Long native tasks require a supported execution adapter with physical resource admission and confirmed cleanup; the generic worker's lease alone is insufficient.</p>
